Chapter: UefiReadKeyNoFirmware

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 With no UEFI stub beneath us there is no system table at any address, and
 after ExitBootServices there is none on real firmware either. A helper that
 cannot reach firmware must SAY SO rather than jump into it: an earlier body
 took a function pointer out of page-table entry zero and called it, which
 faulted on every boot in every mode and went unfound for months.

 0 is the idle answer, not -1. uefi-read-key reads the PS/2 key cell the IRQ1
 handler fills from port 0x60, and an empty cell reads back 0, which is what
 poll-key-decode in Foreword chapter KeyInput already tests for.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    let k = uefi-read-key
    in act
      print-line-uni ("scan=" & show k)
    end
    if k == 0 then print-line-uni "PASS no-firmware read is idle and returns"
    else print-line-uni "FAIL expected idle 0 with no UEFI firmware"
  end
